# Every cross-document part whose framework is known on both sides, beside whether the
# composition cites an instrument. One row per such part, `violates` where the frameworks differ
# and nothing is cited.
#
# Crossing a regime boundary needs a reconciliation
#   The rule: cross a regime boundary and you must say what reconciles it. It is the exact
#   analogue of checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor, and for the same reason in its words:
#   somebody is asserting two things are interchangeable, and an assertion needs a filer. There
#   the assertion is that two units may be added and `asrt:factor` carries it; here it is that
#   two frameworks' figures may be added, and `asrt:Composition/citation` carries the instrument
#   and the clause. `merge-group-composition` composes IFRS-2026 out of `us-gaap` and `NCRF-PE`,
#   which is what consolidation is, and it cites IFRS-10 B86.
#
# A pair that named no framework is excluded
#   That is not a technicality. `IS DISTINCT FROM` calls two NULLs equal, so a fixture that never
#   stated a regime would read as "no crossing" and pass silently. It is not passing; it was
#   never asked.
#
# Why a framework is missing is typed, and this rule does not need it
#   `framework_absent` in composition/regimes.sqlc says whether nobody looked or no framework
#   applies. Either way there is no framework to cross into, so the crossing is not known and the
#   part is outside this rule's population; epistemics/absences.sqlc reports which it was.
#
# No schema change was needed to ask
#   The table this reads needed no schema change to be asked about. A crossing sits in the corpus
#   whether or not a relation reads it, which is why the write side is the only direction that
#   finds a fact nothing queries.
#
# Self-declared frameworks, not the composer's assignment
#   `asrt:part/asrt:layer/asrt:regime` names which of the composition's own regimes each part
#   comes under. This rule does not read it: it compares two documents' self-declared
#   frameworks, not the composer's assignment. Read `composition/regime_crossings.sqlc`'s header
#   before quoting this rule as being about the composer's claim, because it is not.
-- composition/regime_crossings.sqlc; conformance rule "a part crossing a regime boundary files what reconciles it".
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT x.composition AS filing, x.composed_layer AS layer,
           (x.crosses AND x.instrument IS NULL) AS violates,
           CASE WHEN NOT x.crosses
                THEN format('`%s` composes `%s/%s` inside %s', x.composed_layer,
                            x.part_filing, x.part_layer, x.composed_framework)
                WHEN x.instrument IS NOT NULL
                THEN format('`%s` crosses %s to %s, reconciled by %s %s', x.composed_layer,
                            x.part_framework, x.composed_framework, x.instrument, x.clause)
                ELSE format('`%s` crosses %s to %s and cites no instrument', x.composed_layer,
                            x.part_framework, x.composed_framework)
           END AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/regime_crossings.sqlc)
    ) x
    WHERE x.crossing_known
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'regime_crossing_without_a_citation'
